WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.301 --> 00:00:03.188
(whooshing)

00:00:03.188 --> 00:00:07.021
- Amjazzy96, how do we
communicate with people

00:00:07.997 --> 00:00:11.499
who are difficult to work
with when we have to?

00:00:11.499 --> 00:00:13.250
- Oh.
- What do you think?

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:16.917
- Wow, yeah, now, god,
there's loads of like

00:00:17.932 --> 00:00:21.385
behavioral psychology on
this, and I guess hey amjazzy

00:00:21.385 --> 00:00:23.539
it sounds like it's an environment.

00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:26.789
With customers, I'll be
honest, I never find them hard

00:00:26.789 --> 00:00:29.727
to communicate, especially if
we talk about social media.

00:00:29.727 --> 00:00:32.327
It sounds like when you
get in the situation,

00:00:32.327 --> 00:00:35.461
it might not be for you guys,
but if you got a relationship

00:00:35.461 --> 00:00:38.544
that's a bit prickly with your
boss or something like that,

00:00:38.544 --> 00:00:42.775
then that is a position
that can be a bit feisty.

00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:45.414
So I think it comes back
to some of the basics

00:00:45.414 --> 00:00:48.257
Evan has touched on, is
number one, we kind of have

00:00:48.257 --> 00:00:52.424
to put ourselves at the
center of the conversation,

00:00:53.304 --> 00:00:56.799
and put ourselves in their
shoes, and then ask ourselves

00:00:56.799 --> 00:00:59.973
what is it they want and
why are they behaving

00:00:59.973 --> 00:01:02.004
the way they are?

00:01:02.004 --> 00:01:04.274
'Cause normally things are pretty logical.

00:01:04.274 --> 00:01:07.045
And then the other
thing that I touched on,

00:01:07.045 --> 00:01:09.201
all the sort of behavioral
psychology stuff,

00:01:09.201 --> 00:01:11.966
is reality is the beauty of the world is

00:01:11.966 --> 00:01:14.177
everyone's got a different personality.

00:01:14.177 --> 00:01:16.902
And the reality is
different personality types,

00:01:16.902 --> 00:01:18.407
some get on with each other,

00:01:18.407 --> 00:01:21.675
some want to go to a Blue
Jays game all the time

00:01:21.675 --> 00:01:23.745
and they want to hang out an drink beer,

00:01:23.745 --> 00:01:25.167
some people don't want to do that.

00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:27.009
Some people want to listen to music.

00:01:27.009 --> 00:01:28.511
Some people want to read books.

00:01:28.511 --> 00:01:32.347
And therefore, I just think
we've got to accept that

00:01:32.347 --> 00:01:35.071
one of the beauties in life
is we're totally different.

00:01:35.071 --> 00:01:39.536
And I think the tolerance
of that is definitely good.

00:01:39.536 --> 00:01:42.791
Now if it all gets tough,
I think that there's loads

00:01:42.791 --> 00:01:46.383
of stuff you can do in
terms of go to a mentor.

00:01:46.383 --> 00:01:49.024
Ask someone independent,
kind of this is happening,

00:01:49.024 --> 00:01:49.922
this is the situation.

00:01:49.922 --> 00:01:50.815
It could be with a customer.

00:01:50.815 --> 00:01:53.050
It could be with one of
your work colleagues,

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:56.054
someone who works for
you as an entrepreneur.

00:01:56.054 --> 00:01:58.817
And particularly if you're an entrepreneur

00:01:58.817 --> 00:02:01.497
and you're asking someone
in the team to do something

00:02:01.497 --> 00:02:03.984
and it's kind of not landing
or it's poor quality,

00:02:03.984 --> 00:02:05.728
or they're letting customers down,

00:02:05.728 --> 00:02:07.845
you know I can understand that hurts.

00:02:07.845 --> 00:02:12.359
And then 'cause what we said
before, it's very personal.

00:02:12.359 --> 00:02:13.192
It's very personal.

00:02:13.192 --> 00:02:15.689
You guys sacrifice a
lot, you work long hours,

00:02:15.689 --> 00:02:17.478
you pursue your dreams,
and you want everybody

00:02:17.478 --> 00:02:20.528
to pull the same level of weight as you do

00:02:20.528 --> 00:02:24.390
in terms of that idea that
you're trying to get to market.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:26.547
So there's other ways to help.

00:02:26.547 --> 00:02:30.566
So I'd go first to mentorship,
and then ultimately

00:02:30.566 --> 00:02:35.162
if you work out what is
the clock that's ticking

00:02:35.162 --> 00:02:39.442
within the person that's
being challenging,

00:02:39.442 --> 00:02:42.456
I'd always advocate being
honest with people and say

00:02:42.456 --> 00:02:44.008
you know I don't think there's anything.

00:02:44.008 --> 00:02:45.797
Someone taught me once,

00:02:45.797 --> 00:02:47.710
yeah, there's probably
no bad people around.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:52.266
It's just sometimes the
behaviors really upset us.

00:02:52.266 --> 00:02:55.524
And I think if you sat down
with someone and said look,

00:02:55.524 --> 00:02:57.687
you're not a bad person.

00:02:57.687 --> 00:03:00.368
For whatever reason, we're not getting on.

00:03:00.368 --> 00:03:02.848
I think broadly we want the same things.

00:03:02.848 --> 00:03:04.611
I want to build this company.

00:03:04.611 --> 00:03:06.922
I want to be the next Google.

00:03:06.922 --> 00:03:10.460
I want everybody in the
company to follow this dream.

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:11.518
Let's just be honest with each other.

00:03:11.518 --> 00:03:13.756
What's not clicking, what's not working?

00:03:13.756 --> 00:03:15.908
'Cause you know I'm not perfect.

00:03:15.908 --> 00:03:19.163
I think this is where you can
use a bit of self-deprecation

00:03:19.163 --> 00:03:21.113
and share a bit of vulnerability.

00:03:21.113 --> 00:03:23.113
I'm not perfect, but what is it

00:03:23.113 --> 00:03:24.580
that's getting in the way here

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:25.897
'cause we all want the same things,

00:03:25.897 --> 00:03:27.523
but we're bumping into each other.

00:03:27.523 --> 00:03:31.690
So what can we do to just,
as they say in Canada,

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:33.234
get that moose on the table.

00:03:33.234 --> 00:03:36.243
Get that moose on the table
and then we can break it

00:03:36.243 --> 00:03:39.864
into a thousand pieces
and we can consume it

00:03:39.864 --> 00:03:42.058
and sort it out and move on.

00:03:42.058 --> 00:03:43.155
Yeah, I don't know.

00:03:43.155 --> 00:03:43.988
Evan, any ideas?

00:03:43.988 --> 00:03:47.430
- There it is again, that
customization to the audience.

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:48.853
I love it.

00:03:48.853 --> 00:03:52.028
I mean I agree with
everything Stephen's saying.

00:03:52.028 --> 00:03:55.118
I almost want to just copy and
paste, and I would encourage

00:03:55.118 --> 00:03:57.195
you guys to watch that clip back again.

00:03:57.195 --> 00:04:00.505
Those three points he
made were really good.

00:04:00.505 --> 00:04:03.923
The only thing I would add
to it is I go into every

00:04:03.923 --> 00:04:07.579
situation trying to take
full responsibility.

00:04:07.579 --> 00:04:10.769
And I think if you
abdicate responsibilities

00:04:10.769 --> 00:04:13.270
is when you start to
lose in all situations.

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:16.358
But here, as an example, as
soon as you label somebody

00:04:16.358 --> 00:04:19.527
difficult, you're saying
it's their fault, right,

00:04:19.527 --> 00:04:20.582
it's their problem.

00:04:20.582 --> 00:04:23.058
They're the one who's hard to
deal with, they're difficult,

00:04:23.058 --> 00:04:25.905
and that reduces some of
the responsibility on you

00:04:25.905 --> 00:04:27.771
to navigate the situation.

00:04:27.771 --> 00:04:29.451
Where if you just play back
that last piece Stephen

00:04:29.451 --> 00:04:32.352
was talking about is that look,
everybody is a human being.

00:04:32.352 --> 00:04:34.666
There's no bad people,
they just have different

00:04:34.666 --> 00:04:37.353
circumstances, different
upbringings, different values.

00:04:37.353 --> 00:04:39.506
And if you take the
responsibility to say I want

00:04:39.506 --> 00:04:42.761
to navigate this, I want
to make it a success,

00:04:42.761 --> 00:04:44.345
put it on yourself to figure it out,

00:04:44.345 --> 00:04:46.212
then just going in with
that different mindset

00:04:46.212 --> 00:04:49.469
I think could really help
you have more success

00:04:49.469 --> 00:04:51.070
in the conversation.

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:53.319
On top of everything else
you've been talking about,

00:04:53.319 --> 00:04:55.147
getting a mentor, trying
to understand them,

00:04:55.147 --> 00:04:57.177
put yourself in their
shoes, it was really great.

00:04:57.177 --> 00:04:59.407
See, I think I might have to
watch back that answer too.

00:04:59.407 --> 00:05:00.830
I'm going to bookmark that one.

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:03.831
I like that one, that was real good.

